man governor of Yemen, Hasan Pasha. The mosque fell into disrepair after the Ottomans were driven out of Yemen in 1626 but was fully restored when the Ottomans recaptured Sana'a in 1878.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Al-Bakiriyya Mosque","unesco":true,"photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Jemen1988-153_hg.jpg/1280px-Jemen1988-153_hg.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Jemen1988-153_hg.jpg/500px-Jemen1988-153_hg.jpg","w":1200,"h":757,"author":"H. Grobe","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jemen1988-153_hg.jpg"}},{"id":"sanaa_al_mahdi_mosque","areaId":"sanaa","name":"Al-Mahdi Mosque","category":"monument","tags":["history","photo"],"location":{"lat":15.353,"lng":44.2092},"visitMinutes":45,"priceLevel":0,"photoSeed":"sanaa_al_mahdi_mosque","tickets":[],"signals":{"commonsDepicts":1,"commonsNearby":2,"wikiPageviews":78,"wikidataSitelinks":10,"fetchedAt":"2026-10-20"},"hashtags":["almahdimosque","sanaa"],"hours":{"week":[{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"},{"open":"09:00","close":"18:00"}],"note":"Typical hours — check the official site"},"summary":"The Mosque of the Dome of the Mahdi or Al-Mahdi Mosque is one of the historical mosques in the historic old city of Sana'a, Yemen. It forms a part of UNESCO World Heritage Site Old City of Sana'a. It is located in the Al-Kareem Al-Mahdi neighborhood in the western Sarar district.","summarySource":"wikipedia","wikipediaTitle":"Al-Mahdi Mosque, Sanaa","unesco":true,"photo":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Mosque_in_Sana%27a.jpg/1280px-Mosque_in_Sana%27a.jpg","thumb":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Mosque_in_Sana%27a.jpg/500px-Mos